ame.\n\nSo, we conclude that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch of scale factor $4$ in the $x$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/779a10caf7c07cbe4b5891240892db5433adc0f9f8895192a3a8bed5f9301126.png)" 13194139535300 true] [17592186105540 1100 "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ of $\\,\\mathbb{R}^2.$ If $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{i})=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{j})=\\mathbf{j}$ then, what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}?$" 13194139535300 true] [17592186105540 1100 "Consider the standard basis $\\{\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ of $\\,\\mathbb{R}^2$. If $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{i})=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{j})=\\mathbf{j}$ then, what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$?" 13194139535300 false] [17592186105540 1145 "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/c28904112781381da0083548d8c2dbbf.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{i})=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{j})=\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nwe can draw the image of the square under the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/e46ae3a679baae89ff308c327360ee8e.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a vertical shear with a shear factor of $2$." 13194139535300 false] [17592186105540 1145 "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/db15a65fd944c24ea2545baea08e6a0789b749295f623d8d4fdc2f07a37d4b73.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{i})=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{j})=\\mathbf{j},$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/1e5310f50dab35bc25447af9b37d972e11eda0afee98ac99b87a819ba62f56a1.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a vertical shear with a shear factor of $2.$" 13194139535300 true] [17592186105541 1100 "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/e335abcb649dae3ed9ade01017a23998614c3d0ff5a0898eb9b4d657f68e8b62.png)" 13194139535300 true] [17592186105541 1100 "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535300 false] [17592186105541 1145 "We can find the images of the standard basis vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ under the action of  $\\mathbf T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ T(j) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=2i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a *horizontal* shear with a shear factor of $2.$" 13194139535300 true] [17592186105541 1145 "We can find the images of the standard basis vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(i) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = i \\\\\nT(j) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = 2i + j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a *horizontal* shear with a shear factor of $2$." 13194139535300 false] [17592186105542 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/611f958a7bd7475e37f743ef86affde7754195fe2c9ad414e628c17a09d47e38.png)\n\nLet ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/6231eb09a75376d694a6f47c47cf5a26aab4a32c28b2799f7e1f0825a7017f88.png) be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}.$ What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) under the action of $\\mathbf{T}?$" 13194139535300 true] [17592186105542 1100 "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$. What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/8e226d694c513c348b7136c96c29a52b.png)" 13194139535300 false] [17592186105542 1145 "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a vertical shear of shear factor $-1$. Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/6c7707ce3c667c5692b4312d72bc9578.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the parallelogram shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\be